1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,857
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,037 --> 00:00:04,132
Here's something that surprises a lot of

3
00:00:04,132 --> 00:00:04,498
people.

4
00:00:04,678 --> 00:00:07,081
Some of the richest people in America

5
00:00:07,081 --> 00:00:08,834
can pay a lower tax rate on

6
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:10,523
their income than a nurse,

7
00:00:10,523 --> 00:00:12,341
a teacher or an electrician.

8
00:00:12,521 --> 00:00:13,933
And most of the time,

9
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:15,412
it's completely legal.

10
00:00:15,592 --> 00:00:17,821
It's not about secret offshore tricks.

11
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:19,747
It's about how the tax code treats

12
00:00:19,747 --> 00:00:21,031
different kinds of money.

13
00:00:21,211 --> 00:00:23,210
Today I'll show you how it works,

14
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:25,209
and which of those same rules you

15
00:00:25,209 --> 00:00:26,238
can use yourself.

16
00:00:26,588 --> 00:00:27,877
Here's the key idea.

17
00:00:28,057 --> 00:00:29,185
In the United States,

18
00:00:29,185 --> 00:00:31,548
money earned from working is generally taxed

19
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:33,589
at higher rates than money earned from

20
00:00:33,589 --> 00:00:35,683
owning investments held for more than a

21
00:00:35,683 --> 00:00:35,952
year.

22
00:00:36,132 --> 00:00:38,524
Top federal rates on wages reach thirty

23
00:00:38,524 --> 00:00:39,383
seven percent.

24
00:00:39,563 --> 00:00:42,097
Top rates on long term capital gains

25
00:00:42,097 --> 00:00:43,434
are twenty percent,

26
00:00:43,434 --> 00:00:46,180
plus a three point eight percent surtax

27
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:47,376
for high earners.

28
00:00:47,726 --> 00:00:49,898
That single difference explains a lot.

29
00:00:50,078 --> 00:00:53,141
Most people's income comes from a paycheck,

30
00:00:53,141 --> 00:00:55,349
so most of their money is taxed

31
00:00:55,349 --> 00:00:57,130
at ordinary income rates,

32
00:00:57,130 --> 00:00:58,483
plus payroll taxes.

33
00:00:58,663 --> 00:01:00,912
The very wealthy usually get most of

34
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:03,972
their income from investments and businesses they

35
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:06,158
own, which are often taxed at lower

36
00:01:06,158 --> 00:01:08,468
rates, and sometimes not taxed at all

37
00:01:08,468 --> 00:01:09,530
until much later.

38
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:11,639
Same country, same tax code,

39
00:01:11,639 --> 00:01:14,739
very different results depending on what kind

40
00:01:14,739 --> 00:01:15,980
of money you have.

41
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:18,721
We'll explore this from three angles.

42
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:20,352
First, the numbers.

43
00:01:20,532 --> 00:01:23,055
The specific rules that let wealthy people

44
00:01:23,055 --> 00:01:24,015
pay lower rates.

45
00:01:24,195 --> 00:01:26,239
Second, the psychology.

46
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:28,803
How the wealthy think about taxes differently

47
00:01:28,803 --> 00:01:29,704
from most people.

48
00:01:29,884 --> 00:01:31,463
And third, the playbook.

49
00:01:31,643 --> 00:01:34,521
The legal tax advantages ordinary people can

50
00:01:34,521 --> 00:01:36,090
use, starting this year.

51
00:01:36,270 --> 00:01:37,617
Important reminder.

52
00:01:37,797 --> 00:01:39,762
This is general education,

53
00:01:39,762 --> 00:01:40,897
not tax advice.

54
00:01:41,077 --> 00:01:42,690
Tax laws change often,

55
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,817
and your situation is unique,

56
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:47,898
so check with a qualified tax professional

57
00:01:47,898 --> 00:01:48,925
before acting.

58
00:01:49,274 --> 00:01:50,819
Let's start with the numbers.

59
00:01:50,999 --> 00:01:52,806
And the first lesson is about the

60
00:01:52,806 --> 00:01:53,901
two kinds of income.

61
00:01:54,251 --> 00:01:55,029
Our numbers.

62
00:01:55,209 --> 00:01:57,761
Tax rates and account rules come from

63
00:01:57,761 --> 00:02:01,003
the IRS and the Social Security Administration.

64
00:02:01,183 --> 00:02:03,422
Research on the taxes paid by the

65
00:02:03,422 --> 00:02:07,153
wealthiest Americans comes from reporting by ProPublica

66
00:02:07,153 --> 00:02:09,596
and from analysis by the White House

67
00:02:09,596 --> 00:02:12,513
Council of Economic Advisers and the Office

68
00:02:12,513 --> 00:02:14,209
of Management and Budget.

69
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:17,665
Warren Buffett's figures come from his 2011

70
00:02:17,665 --> 00:02:19,265
New York Times op ed.

71
00:02:19,445 --> 00:02:22,235
Rules and thresholds change every year,

72
00:02:22,235 --> 00:02:24,739
so always check the latest numbers.

73
00:02:25,089 --> 00:02:25,786
Angle one.

74
00:02:25,966 --> 00:02:26,663
The numbers.

75
00:02:26,843 --> 00:02:29,044
Seven rules in the tax code that

76
00:02:29,044 --> 00:02:31,795
work very differently for people who own

77
00:02:31,795 --> 00:02:32,276
assets.

78
00:02:32,626 --> 00:02:33,683
Lesson number one.

79
00:02:33,863 --> 00:02:35,808
The tax code treats two kinds of

80
00:02:35,808 --> 00:02:36,963
income differently.

81
00:02:37,312 --> 00:02:39,795
Ordinary income includes wages,

82
00:02:39,795 --> 00:02:43,798
salaries, tips, interest and short term investment

83
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:44,278
gains.

84
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:46,466
It's taxed on a progressive scale,

85
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:49,006
with federal rates ranging from ten percent

86
00:02:49,006 --> 00:02:51,014
up to thirty seven percent for the

87
00:02:51,014 --> 00:02:51,959
highest earners.

88
00:02:52,139 --> 00:02:53,190
The more you earn,

89
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:55,410
the higher the rate on each additional

90
00:02:55,410 --> 00:02:55,819
dollar.

91
00:02:56,168 --> 00:02:57,784
Long term capital gains,

92
00:02:57,784 --> 00:03:00,745
meaning profits on investments held for more

93
00:03:00,745 --> 00:03:01,553
than a year,

94
00:03:01,553 --> 00:03:04,111
and qualified dividends are taxed on a

95
00:03:04,111 --> 00:03:05,793
separate, lower schedule.

96
00:03:05,973 --> 00:03:07,605
Federal rates are zero,

97
00:03:07,605 --> 00:03:09,449
fifteen or twenty percent,

98
00:03:09,449 --> 00:03:11,790
depending on your taxable income.

99
00:03:11,970 --> 00:03:14,283
High earners may also owe a three

100
00:03:14,283 --> 00:03:17,508
point eight percent net investment income tax.

101
00:03:17,857 --> 00:03:20,186
So imagine two people who each have

102
00:03:20,186 --> 00:03:22,447
one million dollars of income in a

103
00:03:22,447 --> 00:03:22,780
year.

104
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:24,794
One earns it as salary.

105
00:03:24,974 --> 00:03:26,858
The other earns it as long term

106
00:03:26,858 --> 00:03:28,864
capital gains from selling stock.

107
00:03:29,044 --> 00:03:31,586
The salary earner faces the top ordinary

108
00:03:31,586 --> 00:03:32,794
rate on much of it.

109
00:03:32,974 --> 00:03:35,356
The investor faces a top rate of

110
00:03:35,356 --> 00:03:37,664
twenty percent plus the surtax.

111
00:03:37,844 --> 00:03:40,493
The investor's tax bill can be dramatically

112
00:03:40,493 --> 00:03:40,863
lower.

113
00:03:41,212 --> 00:03:43,968
Lawmakers have argued for decades about whether

114
00:03:43,968 --> 00:03:44,730
this is fair.

115
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:48,175
Supporters say lower rates encourage investment and

116
00:03:48,175 --> 00:03:50,799
avoid taxing money that was already taxed

117
00:03:50,799 --> 00:03:52,271
at the corporate level.

118
00:03:52,451 --> 00:03:54,982
Critics say it mostly benefits the wealthy.

119
00:03:55,162 --> 00:03:56,452
Either way, it's the law,

120
00:03:56,452 --> 00:03:58,618
and understanding it helps you make better

121
00:03:58,618 --> 00:04:00,166
decisions with your own money.

122
00:04:00,515 --> 00:04:01,735
Lesson number two.

123
00:04:01,915 --> 00:04:03,911
Growth isn't taxed until you sell.

124
00:04:04,261 --> 00:04:04,772
In the U.

125
00:04:04,952 --> 00:04:05,359
S.

126
00:04:05,539 --> 00:04:07,882
tax system, you generally owe tax on

127
00:04:07,882 --> 00:04:10,355
an investment gain only when you sell,

128
00:04:10,355 --> 00:04:12,632
which is called realizing the gain.

129
00:04:12,812 --> 00:04:14,229
If you buy a stock for ten

130
00:04:14,229 --> 00:04:16,190
thousand dollars and it grows to one

131
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:18,423
hundred thousand, you owe nothing on that

132
00:04:18,423 --> 00:04:20,765
ninety thousand dollar gain until you sell.

133
00:04:20,945 --> 00:04:22,358
On paper you're richer,

134
00:04:22,358 --> 00:04:23,647
but for tax purposes,

135
00:04:23,647 --> 00:04:25,183
nothing has happened yet.

136
00:04:25,532 --> 00:04:26,807
For the very wealthy,

137
00:04:26,807 --> 00:04:28,809
most of their fortune is often in

138
00:04:28,809 --> 00:04:31,175
stock of companies they founded or own.

139
00:04:31,355 --> 00:04:33,361
That stock can grow by billions of

140
00:04:33,361 --> 00:04:34,777
dollars without any tax,

141
00:04:34,777 --> 00:04:36,370
as long as they don't sell.

142
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:40,087
In 2021, ProPublica analyzed IRS data and

143
00:04:40,087 --> 00:04:44,142
reported that the twenty five richest Americans

144
00:04:44,142 --> 00:04:47,592
paid federal income taxes equal to about

145
00:04:47,592 --> 00:04:51,043
three point four percent of their wealth

146
00:04:51,043 --> 00:04:53,199
growth from 2014 to 2018.

147
00:04:53,549 --> 00:04:55,413
That figure is controversial,

148
00:04:55,413 --> 00:04:58,176
because it compares taxes to wealth growth,

149
00:04:58,176 --> 00:04:59,590
not to taxable income,

150
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:02,418
and wealth growth isn't income under current

151
00:05:02,418 --> 00:05:02,675
law.

152
00:05:02,855 --> 00:05:04,603
But it shows how much of the

153
00:05:04,603 --> 00:05:07,287
richest people's gains can stay untaxed for

154
00:05:07,287 --> 00:05:07,661
years.

155
00:05:07,841 --> 00:05:10,910
A separate 2021 White House analysis estimated

156
00:05:10,910 --> 00:05:13,845
the wealthiest four hundred families paid an

157
00:05:13,845 --> 00:05:16,847
average federal individual income tax rate of

158
00:05:16,847 --> 00:05:19,916
about eight percent when unrealized gains were

159
00:05:19,916 --> 00:05:20,450
counted.

160
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,690
The lesson for regular investors is simple.

161
00:05:23,870 --> 00:05:26,314
Holding investments for the long term,

162
00:05:26,314 --> 00:05:28,243
instead of constantly selling,

163
00:05:28,243 --> 00:05:30,879
lets your money grow without being slowed

164
00:05:30,879 --> 00:05:32,230
down by yearly taxes.

165
00:05:32,410 --> 00:05:34,409
It's the same rule the wealthy use,

166
00:05:34,409 --> 00:05:36,408
and it's available to anyone with a

167
00:05:36,408 --> 00:05:37,437
brokerage account.

168
00:05:37,786 --> 00:05:39,017
Lesson number three.

169
00:05:39,197 --> 00:05:40,811
Borrowing instead of selling.

170
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:43,342
If the wealthy don't sell their assets,

171
00:05:43,342 --> 00:05:45,131
how do they pay for their lives?

172
00:05:45,311 --> 00:05:47,169
One strategy is borrowing.

173
00:05:47,349 --> 00:05:49,659
Banks are often willing to lend large

174
00:05:49,659 --> 00:05:51,345
amounts to wealthy clients,

175
00:05:51,345 --> 00:05:53,593
using their stock or other assets as

176
00:05:53,593 --> 00:05:54,280
collateral.

177
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:55,331
And under U.

178
00:05:55,511 --> 00:05:55,905
S.

179
00:05:56,085 --> 00:05:59,245
tax law, borrowed money isn't income,

180
00:05:59,245 --> 00:06:01,124
so a loan isn't taxed.

181
00:06:01,474 --> 00:06:03,781
This is sometimes called buy,

182
00:06:03,781 --> 00:06:04,736
borrow, die.

183
00:06:04,916 --> 00:06:06,472
Buy assets that grow.

184
00:06:06,652 --> 00:06:09,178
Borrow against them to fund your lifestyle,

185
00:06:09,178 --> 00:06:11,470
often at relatively low interest rates.

186
00:06:11,650 --> 00:06:14,327
Never sell, so you never realize the

187
00:06:14,327 --> 00:06:14,773
gains.

188
00:06:14,953 --> 00:06:16,516
The assets keep growing,

189
00:06:16,516 --> 00:06:18,535
and the loans are repaid later,

190
00:06:18,535 --> 00:06:19,968
often from the estate.

191
00:06:20,319 --> 00:06:22,182
This doesn't work for everyone,

192
00:06:22,182 --> 00:06:23,685
and it carries real risk.

193
00:06:23,865 --> 00:06:25,942
If asset values fall sharply,

194
00:06:25,942 --> 00:06:29,020
lenders can demand more collateral or force

195
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:29,450
sales.

196
00:06:29,630 --> 00:06:31,139
And interest rates matter.

197
00:06:31,319 --> 00:06:33,150
When borrowing costs rise,

198
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:35,684
the strategy becomes more expensive.

199
00:06:35,864 --> 00:06:38,156
It works best for people with enormous

200
00:06:38,156 --> 00:06:40,207
assets relative to their spending.

201
00:06:40,557 --> 00:06:41,733
For ordinary people,

202
00:06:41,733 --> 00:06:44,146
the takeaway isn't to borrow against your

203
00:06:44,146 --> 00:06:44,852
investments.

204
00:06:45,032 --> 00:06:47,313
It's to understand why the wealthy rarely

205
00:06:47,313 --> 00:06:47,981
sell assets.

206
00:06:48,161 --> 00:06:50,580
They know every sale can trigger taxes,

207
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:52,689
so they treat their best assets as

208
00:06:52,689 --> 00:06:53,619
things to keep,

209
00:06:53,619 --> 00:06:55,046
not things to cash out.

210
00:06:55,396 --> 00:06:56,568
Lesson number four.

211
00:06:56,748 --> 00:06:58,257
The step up in basis.

212
00:06:58,608 --> 00:07:00,639
Here's the final piece of buy,

213
00:07:00,639 --> 00:07:01,452
borrow, die.

214
00:07:01,632 --> 00:07:02,806
When someone dies,

215
00:07:02,806 --> 00:07:06,131
their heirs generally receive inherited assets with

216
00:07:06,131 --> 00:07:08,022
a new cost basis equal to the

217
00:07:08,022 --> 00:07:09,782
value on the date of death.

218
00:07:09,962 --> 00:07:12,133
That's called a step up in basis.

219
00:07:12,313 --> 00:07:14,400
All of the gains built up during

220
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:17,792
the original owner's lifetime can effectively escape

221
00:07:17,792 --> 00:07:18,966
capital gains tax.

222
00:07:19,316 --> 00:07:20,349
Here's an example.

223
00:07:20,529 --> 00:07:23,191
Someone buys stock for one hundred thousand

224
00:07:23,191 --> 00:07:23,687
dollars.

225
00:07:23,867 --> 00:07:26,795
Decades later, it's worth five million when

226
00:07:26,795 --> 00:07:27,408
they die.

227
00:07:27,588 --> 00:07:29,659
Their heirs inherit it with a basis

228
00:07:29,659 --> 00:07:30,606
of five million.

229
00:07:30,786 --> 00:07:32,244
If they sell right away,

230
00:07:32,244 --> 00:07:34,249
they may owe little or no capital

231
00:07:34,249 --> 00:07:36,497
gains tax, even though the stock grew

232
00:07:36,497 --> 00:07:38,623
by four point nine million dollars.

233
00:07:38,973 --> 00:07:41,693
Large estates may owe federal estate tax,

234
00:07:41,693 --> 00:07:44,149
but only above a very high exemption,

235
00:07:44,149 --> 00:07:47,068
which was more than thirteen million dollars

236
00:07:47,068 --> 00:07:48,860
per person in recent years,

237
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:51,050
and the amount is set by Congress

238
00:07:51,050 --> 00:07:52,510
and changes over time.

239
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:55,307
Most families never pay federal estate tax

240
00:07:55,307 --> 00:07:55,743
at all.

241
00:07:56,094 --> 00:07:58,589
The step up applies to regular families

242
00:07:58,589 --> 00:07:58,845
too.

243
00:07:59,025 --> 00:08:01,646
A home or stock portfolio passed to

244
00:08:01,646 --> 00:08:04,342
children can also receive a step up.

245
00:08:04,522 --> 00:08:06,579
That's why estate planning isn't just for

246
00:08:06,579 --> 00:08:07,030
the rich.

247
00:08:07,210 --> 00:08:10,066
A simple conversation with an estate attorney

248
00:08:10,066 --> 00:08:11,970
can save heirs a lot of money.

249
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:13,737
Lesson number five.

250
00:08:13,917 --> 00:08:16,297
Payroll taxes hit workers harder.

251
00:08:16,646 --> 00:08:19,696
Most workers pay payroll taxes on top

252
00:08:19,696 --> 00:08:20,849
of income tax.

253
00:08:21,029 --> 00:08:23,851
The Social Security tax is six point

254
00:08:23,851 --> 00:08:25,496
two percent of wages,

255
00:08:25,496 --> 00:08:28,161
and Medicare tax is one point four

256
00:08:28,161 --> 00:08:29,180
five percent.

257
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:31,600
Your employer pays a matching amount.

258
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:33,209
If you're self employed,

259
00:08:33,209 --> 00:08:35,530
you generally pay both halves yourself.

260
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:38,838
But the Social Security portion only applies

261
00:08:38,838 --> 00:08:40,585
up to a yearly wage limit,

262
00:08:40,585 --> 00:08:43,206
which was about one hundred seventy six

263
00:08:43,206 --> 00:08:46,029
thousand dollars in 2025 and usually rises

264
00:08:46,029 --> 00:08:46,701
each year.

265
00:08:46,881 --> 00:08:49,558
Wages above that limit aren't subject to

266
00:08:49,558 --> 00:08:52,302
the six point two percent Social Security

267
00:08:52,302 --> 00:08:52,570
tax.

268
00:08:52,750 --> 00:08:55,338
So a person earning ten million dollars

269
00:08:55,338 --> 00:08:57,395
in salary pays that tax only on

270
00:08:57,395 --> 00:08:58,590
the first portion.

271
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:02,737
Investment income, like capital gains and dividends,

272
00:09:02,737 --> 00:09:05,512
isn't subject to payroll taxes at all,

273
00:09:05,512 --> 00:09:08,578
although high earners may owe the separate

274
00:09:08,578 --> 00:09:12,010
three point eight percent net investment income

275
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:12,303
tax.

276
00:09:12,483 --> 00:09:15,140
For someone whose income is mostly from

277
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:18,206
investments, payroll taxes are a small factor

278
00:09:18,206 --> 00:09:19,228
or none at all.

279
00:09:19,578 --> 00:09:21,860
That means a middle income worker can

280
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:23,587
pay a combined federal rate,

281
00:09:23,587 --> 00:09:24,821
income plus payroll,

282
00:09:24,821 --> 00:09:27,288
that's higher than someone who earns far

283
00:09:27,288 --> 00:09:28,645
more from investments.

284
00:09:28,825 --> 00:09:30,665
It's one of the main reasons people

285
00:09:30,665 --> 00:09:32,347
say the rich can pay lower rates

286
00:09:32,347 --> 00:09:33,504
than the middle class.

287
00:09:33,854 --> 00:09:35,258
Lesson number six.

288
00:09:35,438 --> 00:09:38,167
Business owners are taxed on profit.

289
00:09:38,517 --> 00:09:41,252
An employee is generally taxed on their

290
00:09:41,252 --> 00:09:43,637
gross wages, before most expenses.

291
00:09:43,817 --> 00:09:46,436
A business owner is taxed on profit,

292
00:09:46,436 --> 00:09:48,983
after legitimate business expenses.

293
00:09:49,163 --> 00:09:50,891
Equipment, office costs,

294
00:09:50,891 --> 00:09:52,762
business travel, software,

295
00:09:52,762 --> 00:09:56,073
and employees' salaries can all reduce taxable

296
00:09:56,073 --> 00:09:59,384
profit when they're ordinary and necessary for

297
00:09:59,384 --> 00:10:00,320
the business.

298
00:10:00,671 --> 00:10:03,928
Real estate investors have another powerful tool,

299
00:10:03,928 --> 00:10:04,792
depreciation.

300
00:10:04,972 --> 00:10:07,355
The tax code allows owners to deduct

301
00:10:07,355 --> 00:10:09,804
part of a rental building's cost each

302
00:10:09,804 --> 00:10:12,319
year, even if the property is actually

303
00:10:12,319 --> 00:10:13,378
rising in value.

304
00:10:13,558 --> 00:10:16,958
Depreciation can reduce or even eliminate taxable

305
00:10:16,958 --> 00:10:18,555
rental income on paper,

306
00:10:18,555 --> 00:10:21,122
although some of it may be recaptured

307
00:10:21,122 --> 00:10:22,927
when the property is sold.

308
00:10:23,277 --> 00:10:25,955
Some business owners also qualify for a

309
00:10:25,955 --> 00:10:28,633
deduction on qualified business income,

310
00:10:28,633 --> 00:10:31,242
which can reduce taxes on certain pass

311
00:10:31,242 --> 00:10:32,959
through business profits.

312
00:10:33,139 --> 00:10:34,600
These rules are complex,

313
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:36,244
with limits and conditions,

314
00:10:36,244 --> 00:10:38,557
which is why business owners often pay

315
00:10:38,557 --> 00:10:40,140
for professional tax help.

316
00:10:40,490 --> 00:10:42,208
The key word is legitimate.

317
00:10:42,388 --> 00:10:46,376
Deducting personal expenses as business expenses is

318
00:10:46,376 --> 00:10:48,878
illegal, and the IRS does audit.

319
00:10:49,058 --> 00:10:51,479
But a real side business with real

320
00:10:51,479 --> 00:10:54,541
expenses can give ordinary people access to

321
00:10:54,541 --> 00:10:56,535
some of the same advantages.

322
00:10:56,885 --> 00:10:58,231
Lesson number seven.

323
00:10:58,411 --> 00:10:59,421
Carried interest.

324
00:10:59,772 --> 00:11:02,419
Private equity and hedge fund managers often

325
00:11:02,419 --> 00:11:05,306
get paid partly through something called carried

326
00:11:05,306 --> 00:11:07,713
interest, a share of the fund's profits.

327
00:11:07,893 --> 00:11:09,102
Under current law,

328
00:11:09,102 --> 00:11:12,261
if certain holding period requirements are met,

329
00:11:12,261 --> 00:11:14,345
that share can be taxed as long

330
00:11:14,345 --> 00:11:17,436
term capital gains instead of ordinary income.

331
00:11:17,787 --> 00:11:20,459
Critics argue that carried interest is really

332
00:11:20,459 --> 00:11:21,469
payment for work,

333
00:11:21,469 --> 00:11:23,429
so it should be taxed like wages.

334
00:11:23,609 --> 00:11:26,109
Supporters argue it's a return on risk

335
00:11:26,109 --> 00:11:29,135
and investment, similar to an ownership stake.

336
00:11:29,315 --> 00:11:32,704
Lawmakers have proposed changing it many times,

337
00:11:32,704 --> 00:11:35,517
and in 2017 the required holding period

338
00:11:35,517 --> 00:11:38,113
was extended to three years for many

339
00:11:38,113 --> 00:11:38,545
cases.

340
00:11:38,896 --> 00:11:41,998
Carried interest affects relatively few people,

341
00:11:41,998 --> 00:11:43,912
but it's a clear example of a

342
00:11:43,912 --> 00:11:44,968
broader pattern.

343
00:11:45,148 --> 00:11:47,033
When income can be labeled as a

344
00:11:47,033 --> 00:11:49,466
return on investment instead of a return

345
00:11:49,466 --> 00:11:51,352
on labor, it's often taxed at a

346
00:11:51,352 --> 00:11:52,021
lower rate.

347
00:11:52,371 --> 00:11:53,587
For ordinary people,

348
00:11:53,587 --> 00:11:56,446
the lesson isn't about carried interest itself.

349
00:11:56,626 --> 00:11:58,390
It's that owning assets,

350
00:11:58,390 --> 00:12:00,449
through retirement accounts,

351
00:12:00,449 --> 00:12:03,317
index funds, real estate or a business,

352
00:12:03,317 --> 00:12:06,405
shifts more of your income into categories

353
00:12:06,405 --> 00:12:08,979
the tax code treats more favorably.

354
00:12:09,329 --> 00:12:10,315
That's angle one.

355
00:12:10,495 --> 00:12:12,081
Two kinds of income,

356
00:12:12,081 --> 00:12:14,141
untaxed growth, borrowing,

357
00:12:14,141 --> 00:12:15,806
the step up in basis,

358
00:12:15,806 --> 00:12:17,312
payroll tax limits,

359
00:12:17,312 --> 00:12:20,561
business deductions and carried interest.

360
00:12:20,741 --> 00:12:21,937
Now angle two.

361
00:12:22,117 --> 00:12:23,255
The psychology.

362
00:12:23,605 --> 00:12:25,658
The wealthy don't just have different rules

363
00:12:25,658 --> 00:12:26,136
available.

364
00:12:26,316 --> 00:12:28,080
They think about taxes differently.

365
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,613
Most people deal with taxes once a

366
00:12:30,613 --> 00:12:31,651
year, in April,

367
00:12:31,651 --> 00:12:33,589
and try to get it over with.

368
00:12:33,769 --> 00:12:36,193
Wealthy people treat taxes as a year

369
00:12:36,193 --> 00:12:38,820
round part of every financial decision.

370
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,229
Here are seven ways their thinking differs.

371
00:12:41,579 --> 00:12:42,681
Lesson number eight.

372
00:12:42,861 --> 00:12:45,033
They think in after tax returns.

373
00:12:45,383 --> 00:12:47,197
Most people focus on how much they

374
00:12:47,197 --> 00:12:49,225
earn or what their investments return.

375
00:12:49,405 --> 00:12:51,344
Wealthy people focus on what they keep.

376
00:12:51,524 --> 00:12:53,930
A ten percent return taxed at thirty

377
00:12:53,930 --> 00:12:56,737
seven percent leaves about six point three

378
00:12:56,737 --> 00:12:57,271
percent.

379
00:12:57,451 --> 00:13:00,022
A ten percent return taxed at fifteen

380
00:13:00,022 --> 00:13:02,246
percent leaves eight and a half.

381
00:13:02,426 --> 00:13:04,341
The after tax number is the one

382
00:13:04,341 --> 00:13:05,514
that builds wealth.

383
00:13:05,865 --> 00:13:07,490
This changes decisions.

384
00:13:07,670 --> 00:13:09,392
Where to hold investments,

385
00:13:09,392 --> 00:13:10,253
when to sell,

386
00:13:10,253 --> 00:13:11,312
how to get paid,

387
00:13:11,312 --> 00:13:13,498
whether to take a raise as salary

388
00:13:13,498 --> 00:13:14,160
or equity.

389
00:13:14,340 --> 00:13:17,465
Every choice gets evaluated through the question,

390
00:13:17,465 --> 00:13:19,251
what's the after tax result?

391
00:13:19,601 --> 00:13:22,166
Regular people can adopt this same habit.

392
00:13:22,346 --> 00:13:25,294
When comparing two investments or two jobs,

393
00:13:25,294 --> 00:13:28,172
ask what you'll actually keep after taxes,

394
00:13:28,172 --> 00:13:30,160
not just the headline number.

395
00:13:30,510 --> 00:13:31,276
Try this.

396
00:13:31,456 --> 00:13:33,948
Look at last year's tax return and

397
00:13:33,948 --> 00:13:36,440
calculate your effective tax rate,

398
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:38,933
total tax divided by total income.

399
00:13:39,113 --> 00:13:41,153
Knowing that number makes the cost of

400
00:13:41,153 --> 00:13:41,760
taxes real.

401
00:13:42,110 --> 00:13:43,387
Lesson number nine.

402
00:13:43,567 --> 00:13:44,753
They plan all year,

403
00:13:44,753 --> 00:13:45,877
not just in April.

404
00:13:46,227 --> 00:13:48,240
By the time most people file their

405
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:50,135
taxes, the year is over and most

406
00:13:50,135 --> 00:13:51,497
opportunities are gone.

407
00:13:51,677 --> 00:13:54,680
Retirement contributions, the timing of sales,

408
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:57,095
charitable gifts, business purchases,

409
00:13:57,095 --> 00:13:59,379
all of these usually need to happen

410
00:13:59,379 --> 00:14:01,337
before the year ends to count.

411
00:14:01,687 --> 00:14:04,605
Wealthy families often meet with tax advisors

412
00:14:04,605 --> 00:14:06,680
in the fall to plan moves before

413
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:08,107
December thirty first.

414
00:14:08,287 --> 00:14:10,366
Should they sell some investments at a

415
00:14:10,366 --> 00:14:11,514
loss to offset gains?

416
00:14:11,694 --> 00:14:13,932
Should they make a charitable gift this

417
00:14:13,932 --> 00:14:14,678
year or next?

418
00:14:14,858 --> 00:14:17,162
Should they convert some retirement money to

419
00:14:17,162 --> 00:14:17,528
a Roth?

420
00:14:17,879 --> 00:14:19,841
You don't need a team of advisors

421
00:14:19,841 --> 00:14:21,269
to do a simpler version.

422
00:14:21,449 --> 00:14:23,912
A short review in October or November

423
00:14:23,912 --> 00:14:26,441
can reveal moves that save real money.

424
00:14:26,791 --> 00:14:27,592
Try this.

425
00:14:27,772 --> 00:14:29,926
Put a tax planning reminder in your

426
00:14:29,926 --> 00:14:31,649
calendar for early November.

427
00:14:31,829 --> 00:14:34,162
Check your retirement contributions,

428
00:14:34,162 --> 00:14:36,299
your investment gains and losses,

429
00:14:36,299 --> 00:14:38,308
and any big expenses coming up.

430
00:14:38,658 --> 00:14:39,888
Lesson number ten.

431
00:14:40,068 --> 00:14:42,065
They see good tax advice as an

432
00:14:42,065 --> 00:14:42,797
investment.

433
00:14:43,147 --> 00:14:45,237
Many people try to save money by

434
00:14:45,237 --> 00:14:47,523
doing complicated taxes themselves.

435
00:14:47,703 --> 00:14:50,166
Wealthy people usually see a good tax

436
00:14:50,166 --> 00:14:52,162
professional as an investment.

437
00:14:52,342 --> 00:14:55,022
If a five hundred dollar consultation saves

438
00:14:55,022 --> 00:14:56,892
two thousand dollars in taxes,

439
00:14:56,892 --> 00:14:58,263
that's a great return.

440
00:14:58,613 --> 00:15:00,344
For simple situations,

441
00:15:00,344 --> 00:15:01,839
a regular paycheck,

442
00:15:01,839 --> 00:15:05,852
standard deduction, basic retirement contributions,

443
00:15:05,852 --> 00:15:09,157
tax software or free filing programs often

444
00:15:09,157 --> 00:15:09,944
work fine.

445
00:15:10,124 --> 00:15:11,556
But business income,

446
00:15:11,556 --> 00:15:14,133
rental property, stock compensation,

447
00:15:14,133 --> 00:15:16,639
big life changes or selling a large

448
00:15:16,639 --> 00:15:19,574
investment can justify professional help.

449
00:15:19,925 --> 00:15:22,833
Look for credentials like CPA or enrolled

450
00:15:22,833 --> 00:15:25,033
agent, and ask how they charge.

451
00:15:25,213 --> 00:15:27,540
A good tax professional should save you

452
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:29,748
more than they cost and explain their

453
00:15:29,748 --> 00:15:31,180
recommendations clearly.

454
00:15:31,531 --> 00:15:32,262
Try this.

455
00:15:32,442 --> 00:15:35,249
If your taxes got more complicated this

456
00:15:35,249 --> 00:15:38,200
year, book a one time consultation before

457
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:39,640
year end, not after.

458
00:15:39,991 --> 00:15:41,384
Lesson number eleven.

459
00:15:41,564 --> 00:15:42,764
They focus on owning,

460
00:15:42,764 --> 00:15:43,735
not just earning.

461
00:15:44,085 --> 00:15:45,114
For most people,

462
00:15:45,114 --> 00:15:47,558
wealth building starts and ends with a

463
00:15:47,558 --> 00:15:48,136
paycheck.

464
00:15:48,316 --> 00:15:50,394
But wages are the most heavily taxed

465
00:15:50,394 --> 00:15:52,183
kind of income for most people.

466
00:15:52,363 --> 00:15:55,046
Wealthy people focus on owning things that

467
00:15:55,046 --> 00:15:56,196
grow, like stocks,

468
00:15:56,196 --> 00:15:57,922
businesses and real estate,

469
00:15:57,922 --> 00:16:00,286
whose gains are often taxed later and

470
00:16:00,286 --> 00:16:01,244
at lower rates.

471
00:16:01,595 --> 00:16:03,824
That doesn't mean your paycheck isn't important.

472
00:16:04,004 --> 00:16:04,923
For most people,

473
00:16:04,923 --> 00:16:07,568
it's the engine that makes investing possible.

474
00:16:07,748 --> 00:16:09,398
The goal is to turn part of

475
00:16:09,398 --> 00:16:10,987
each paycheck into assets,

476
00:16:10,987 --> 00:16:12,943
so over time more of your wealth

477
00:16:12,943 --> 00:16:14,226
comes from ownership.

478
00:16:14,577 --> 00:16:17,730
Over decades, someone who consistently invests can

479
00:16:17,730 --> 00:16:19,621
end up with a large portion of

480
00:16:19,621 --> 00:16:22,018
their net worth in assets whose growth

481
00:16:22,018 --> 00:16:23,342
has never been taxed.

482
00:16:23,522 --> 00:16:26,256
That's the same structure the wealthy use,

483
00:16:26,256 --> 00:16:27,818
just at a smaller scale.

484
00:16:28,168 --> 00:16:28,945
Try this.

485
00:16:29,125 --> 00:16:31,090
Look at your net worth and ask

486
00:16:31,090 --> 00:16:33,841
what percentage comes from assets that can

487
00:16:33,841 --> 00:16:36,265
grow, rather than cash or things that

488
00:16:36,265 --> 00:16:36,985
lose value.

489
00:16:37,165 --> 00:16:39,952
Aim to increase that percentage every year.

490
00:16:40,302 --> 00:16:41,172
Quick pause.

491
00:16:41,352 --> 00:16:44,093
If you're learning something about taxes that

492
00:16:44,093 --> 00:16:45,798
nobody taught you in school,

493
00:16:45,798 --> 00:16:48,051
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

494
00:16:48,231 --> 00:16:50,064
It's free, and it helps us keep

495
00:16:50,064 --> 00:16:50,832
making these.

496
00:16:51,012 --> 00:16:52,556
Now, lesson twelve.

497
00:16:52,906 --> 00:16:54,264
Lesson number twelve.

498
00:16:54,444 --> 00:16:55,779
They're patient sellers.

499
00:16:56,129 --> 00:16:58,816
Sell an investment after eleven months and

500
00:16:58,816 --> 00:17:01,312
the gain is generally taxed as ordinary

501
00:17:01,312 --> 00:17:01,759
income.

502
00:17:01,939 --> 00:17:03,523
Wait just over a year,

503
00:17:03,523 --> 00:17:05,898
and it may qualify for lower long

504
00:17:05,898 --> 00:17:07,698
term capital gains rates.

505
00:17:07,878 --> 00:17:09,780
A few weeks of patience can make

506
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:11,860
a real difference in what you keep.

507
00:17:12,211 --> 00:17:16,599
Wealthy investors also avoid unnecessary selling altogether.

508
00:17:16,779 --> 00:17:18,901
Every sale can create a tax bill

509
00:17:18,901 --> 00:17:21,620
that shrinks the amount left to compound.

510
00:17:21,800 --> 00:17:24,209
Holding great assets for decades lets the

511
00:17:24,209 --> 00:17:25,678
full amount keep growing.

512
00:17:26,029 --> 00:17:28,118
Frequent traders face the opposite.

513
00:17:28,298 --> 00:17:30,768
Short term gains taxed at higher rates,

514
00:17:30,768 --> 00:17:31,971
plus trading costs,

515
00:17:31,971 --> 00:17:33,998
can seriously drag down returns,

516
00:17:33,998 --> 00:17:35,961
even when their picks are good.

517
00:17:36,311 --> 00:17:37,193
Try this.

518
00:17:37,373 --> 00:17:40,138
Before selling any investment in a taxable

519
00:17:40,138 --> 00:17:42,639
account, check how long you've held it

520
00:17:42,639 --> 00:17:44,351
and what the tax would be.

521
00:17:44,531 --> 00:17:46,695
Sometimes waiting a bit longer is the

522
00:17:46,695 --> 00:17:47,514
smartest move.

523
00:17:47,865 --> 00:17:49,385
Lesson number thirteen.

524
00:17:49,565 --> 00:17:50,808
They give strategically.

525
00:17:51,158 --> 00:17:54,870
Many wealthy people donate appreciated stock instead

526
00:17:54,870 --> 00:17:55,442
of cash.

527
00:17:55,622 --> 00:17:57,844
If you've held a stock for more

528
00:17:57,844 --> 00:17:59,922
than a year and it has grown,

529
00:17:59,922 --> 00:18:03,004
donating it directly to a qualified charity

530
00:18:03,004 --> 00:18:05,513
can let you avoid capital gains tax

531
00:18:05,513 --> 00:18:06,516
on the growth,

532
00:18:06,516 --> 00:18:07,950
and, if you itemize,

533
00:18:07,950 --> 00:18:10,028
deduct its fair market value,

534
00:18:10,028 --> 00:18:11,318
within IRS limits.

535
00:18:11,669 --> 00:18:13,763
Some also use donor advised funds,

536
00:18:13,763 --> 00:18:16,103
which let them make a large charitable

537
00:18:16,103 --> 00:18:17,643
contribution in one year,

538
00:18:17,643 --> 00:18:19,861
take the deduction that year if they

539
00:18:19,861 --> 00:18:22,756
itemize, and then recommend grants to charities

540
00:18:22,756 --> 00:18:23,371
over time.

541
00:18:23,722 --> 00:18:25,930
People seventy and a half or older

542
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:28,204
can often give directly from an IRA

543
00:18:28,204 --> 00:18:31,777
to charity through a qualified charitable distribution,

544
00:18:31,777 --> 00:18:35,220
which can count toward required minimum distributions

545
00:18:35,220 --> 00:18:37,363
without adding to taxable income.

546
00:18:37,543 --> 00:18:39,625
The rules have specific limits,

547
00:18:39,625 --> 00:18:41,909
so check the current IRS guidance.

548
00:18:42,259 --> 00:18:42,932
Try this.

549
00:18:43,112 --> 00:18:45,013
If you already give to charity,

550
00:18:45,013 --> 00:18:48,262
ask whether giving appreciated investments instead of

551
00:18:48,262 --> 00:18:50,531
cash would make your gift go further.

552
00:18:50,881 --> 00:18:52,355
Lesson number fourteen.

553
00:18:52,535 --> 00:18:54,536
They see taxes as one of their

554
00:18:54,536 --> 00:18:55,670
biggest expenses.

555
00:18:56,020 --> 00:18:57,398
For many households,

556
00:18:57,398 --> 00:18:58,915
taxes, federal, state,

557
00:18:58,915 --> 00:19:01,327
local, payroll, property and sales,

558
00:19:01,327 --> 00:19:03,257
add up to one of the largest

559
00:19:03,257 --> 00:19:04,912
expenses in their lives,

560
00:19:04,912 --> 00:19:06,979
sometimes larger than housing.

561
00:19:07,159 --> 00:19:09,406
Yet most people spend far more time

562
00:19:09,406 --> 00:19:12,999
comparing grocery prices than understanding their taxes.

563
00:19:13,350 --> 00:19:16,018
Wealthy people manage taxes the way they

564
00:19:16,018 --> 00:19:17,552
manage any big expense.

565
00:19:17,732 --> 00:19:19,617
They look for legal ways to reduce

566
00:19:19,617 --> 00:19:21,169
it, plan ahead and track it.

567
00:19:21,349 --> 00:19:23,328
Saving a few percent on a huge

568
00:19:23,328 --> 00:19:26,034
expense can matter more than cutting many

569
00:19:26,034 --> 00:19:26,759
small ones.

570
00:19:27,110 --> 00:19:29,292
This isn't about avoiding your fair share.

571
00:19:29,472 --> 00:19:32,060
It's about using the rules Congress wrote,

572
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,648
many of which were created specifically to

573
00:19:34,648 --> 00:19:36,619
encourage saving for retirement,

